  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Fon\"\nsubtitle: \"Benin\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | Fon communities of southern Benin, represented here by objects from Abomey, Ouidah, and older museum collections. |\n| Where | Southern Benin; the selected objects include court material from Abomey and an altar from Ouidah. |\n| Known for | Royal staffs and appliqué banners · Ancestor altars · Ritual pottery · Metal figures and gongs |\n\n## Overview\n\nThis gallery follows Fon-attributed objects rather than treating the old kingdom of Dahomey as a complete portrait of present-day Fon life. The selected records span court insignia, ancestor remembrance, pottery, jewelry, musical instruments, and sculpture. Their museum labels and collecting histories vary; an object bought at an Abomey market was not necessarily made there.\n\n## Material culture\n\nThe Met identifies *asen* as portable metal altars placed in family or royal shrine houses. People used them to address and remember the dead; their figures and symbols could refer to a particular person's life. One selected royal *asen* came from the Abomey court, while another was made for remembrance in Ouidah. The British Museum's Fon-attributed pottery includes a red vessel recorded for a royal ancestor cult and a two-headed serpent vessel recorded for the cult of Dã. These specific catalogue notes support those uses, not a single purpose for every pot.\n\nRoyal and ceremonial objects take several forms. A bird-headed *récade* in the Toulouse museum is explicitly identified as Fon and described as a sign of the ruler's authority. The British Museum's appliqué banner depicts a Dahomean victory in the reign of King Glélé. Its catalogue calls this particular cloth an early twentieth-century replica made by the traditional Yémadjé makers in Abomey, so it should not be mistaken for a banner carried in that battle. The selected metal figures, beaded ornaments, gongs, drum, and rattle staffs show other forms without assigning them all to the court.\n\nFour separately reviewed Commons photographs show a Fon *récade*, an apron labeled Fon by its museum, a Vodun figure identified as Fon, and a royal or noble costume attributed to Fon by the Brooklyn Museum. They are photographs of objects, not a record of one contemporary performance.\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- [The Met: royal *asen* with bird](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/312286) — Abomey court and ancestor commemoration.\n- [The Met: *asen* from Ouidah](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/312299) — family shrine use and symbolism.\n- [British Museum: royal ancestor vessel](https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/E_Af1951-12-165) — recorded purpose and Abomey collecting group.\n- [British Museum: serpent vessel](https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/E_Af1951-12-172-a) — relief serpent and recorded cult of Dã.\n- [British Museum: appliqué banner](https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/E_Af1982-23-1) — subject and replica attribution.\n- [British Museum: double gong](https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/E_Af1951-12-187) — recorded ceremonial and dance use.\n- [Muséum de Toulouse *récade* photograph and catalogue text](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R%C3%A9cade_MHNT_ETH_AC_450.jpg) — Fon attribution and royal role.\n",
